+\paragraph{Why a fine-tune ends above scratch: the start, not the fine-tune undoing it (2026-10-29).}\label{par:ft_harm}
+Whether fine-tuning undoes something the pretrained model already has, or never had it to start with, where it loses
+to scratch at small $D$ (pars.~\ref{par:nogain}, \ref{par:lowd_ft}, \ref{par:lowd_dyhpo}). Read from the saved
+validation curves, all already home (\code{analysis/transfer/ft\_harm.py}, \code{data/ft\_harm.json},
+Fig.~\ref{fig:ft_harm}): the 8k grid's best fine-tune per rung, probe and $D$, which loses to scratch in $142$ of
+$840$ cells; the first round's runs at the grid's HPs, $79$ of $240$ losing; the DyHPO round's best per method and cell,
+$44$ of $180$ losing (the losing small-$D$ runs are all on $ee\to Z\gamma$, $ud\to ud$ and $u\bar u\to gg$). The
+training loop does not validate at step $0$; the start is the read-out job's (\code{tools/linear\_readout.py}) for
+rungs $1$, $7$, $9$ on the five small-$D$ probes at $D=10$ to $10^{2.5}$: no run resets the head, so a default, L2-SP
+or frozen-block run starts at the pretrained network as is, and a linear probe then fine-tune run at the ridge
+read-out. The read-out is also what the frozen trunk holds under a refitted linear head. Each losing run with a known
+start falls in one of three classes against scratch's best: (i) the start and the read-out both above it, a bad
+start; (ii) the start at or below it, a start the fine-tune undoes; (iii) the start above it but the read-out at or
+below, the trunk holding what the run does not find. (i) is nearly all of them: $77$ of the first round's losing
+runs ($1$ in (ii), $1$ in (iii)), $42$ of the DyHPO round's ($2$ in (iii)), and all $13$ grid cells with a known start
+($129$ grid cells have none: other rungs, probes or $D$). The one (ii) run is the linear-probe run on $ee\to Z\gamma$,
+rung $7$, $D=10^{1.5}$ already noted in par.~\ref{par:lowd_ft}: its first validation, a hundredth of the way into the run, is $4.4\times$
+above its start and its best $2.9\times$; the (iii) runs are L2-SP on that cell and on $ud\to ud$, rung $7$, $D=10^2$.
+Over the $136$ losing runs with a known start the read-out sits above scratch's best in $132$, by a median $1.09$
+decades (the pretrained network as is starts $3.96$ decades above, the linear-probe runs $0.90$). The sign of the
+read-out against scratch nearly decides the loss: where it is at or below scratch the fine-tune loses in $2$ of $100$
+runs of the first round, $2$ of $75$ of the DyHPO round and $0$ of $25$ grid cells; where it is above, in $77$ of
+$140$, $42$ of $105$ and $13$ of $35$. A start that does not hold the probe is needed for a loss, not enough for one.
+Fine-tuning that ends above its own frozen read-out, worse than the pretrained trunk with only its head refitted (a
+count that favours the fine-tune: its checkpoint is chosen on validation, the read-out's penalty on the training
+events), is the minority: $10$ of the grid's $60$ cells, $46$ of $240$ first-round runs and $23$ of $180$ DyHPO-round
+runs, mostly on $ee\to Z\gamma$ ($4$ of $12$, $23$ of $48$, $11$ of $36$); the median of $\log_{10}$ of the fine-tune over its read-out
+is $-0.79$, $-0.52$ and $-0.74$. Nor do the losing curves climb: the last validation is twice or more above the
+best in $15$ of the $142$ grid runs, $8$ of $79$ and $5$ of $44$; the best checkpoint sits at a median $0.82$, $0.64$ and
+$0.37$ of the run's length, in the last tenth for $66$, $29$ and $17$ runs. So where fine-tuning loses to scratch at small $D$
+it is not undoing a start better than scratch: the pretrained trunk does not hold the probe at scratch's level, and
+from there the fine-tune flattens out above scratch's best, which scratch from a random start reaches. On
+$ee\to Z\gamma$ the fine-tune also loses part of what the trunk holds in about a third of its runs, a second, smaller
+effect. Not read: whether that start fails through what the weights encode (a basin, par.~\ref{par:nogain}'s rung $6$
+against rung $7$) or through their statistics (scale and conditioning, lost plasticity); the curves cannot tell.
